  "modernization": {
    "modern_text": "Sir, Williamsburg July 20th 1779. I take the liberty of begging leave of your Excellency to forward the enclosed by the first flag which may happen to be going into New York. They are addressed to a good man in distress which I am sure will apologize with you for my asking your intervention. I am with the greatest respect your Excellency's most obedient [?] & most [?] humble servant [?]. His Excellency Th: Jefferson Genl: Washington (Copy)",
    "plain_english": "Sir, writing from Williamsburg on July 20, 1779, I am asking your Excellency for permission to send the enclosed letters through the next flag of truce going into New York. They are meant for a good man who is in trouble, which I am confident will serve as an apology to you for my request for your help. I remain, with the utmost respect, your Excellency's most obedient and humble servant. His Excellency Thomas Jefferson, General Washington. (Copy)",
    "summary": "The author writes to General Washington from Williamsburg on July 20, 1779, asking him to forward enclosed letters through a flag of truce to New York for a man in distress. The writer apologizes for the intrusion and expresses great respect.",
    "glossary": [
      {
        "original": "obt.",
        "modern": "obedient",
        "category": "abbreviation",
        "explanation": "An 18th-century abbreviation for the word obedient, commonly used in formal letter closings."
      },
      {
